  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate change has worsened the frequency and severity of weather-related losses, adding to farmers&#x2019; uncertainty. Australia assisted in:
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Providing tools to coffee farmers to rehabilitate their trees.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Providing drying racks to coffee farmers to cope with excessive rains.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Introducing climate-smart practices for pig farmers to adapt to climate change impacts.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Helping seaweed farmers adapt to climate change through adaptation measures.
&#x200d;
Through this work, 2,840 coffee and pig farmers received access to climate-resilient inputs or tools. The supporting evidence include list of farmers receiving the rehabilitation tools as well as training on climate-smart practices through Australian interventions.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">IIn Timor-Leste, limited access to agricultural inputs, such as seeds, fertilizer, and feed, has long constrained productivity for rural and underserved communities. While some farmers rely on locally produced inputs, these are often inefficient, inconsistent, and nutritionally inadequate. 
&#x200d;
To address this, the Market Development Facility (MDF) supported three Timorese agricultural input importers in 2024 to strengthen commercial ties with international manufacturers and expand retail presence beyond Dili. This included establishing 14 new agri-input retail outlets, including three mobile shops, bringing products closer to rural communities. MDF also partnered with a local firm to manufacture quality pig and chicken feed domestically, reducing reliance on imports and improving supply chain resilience.
&#x200d;
Through these interventions, farmers are expected to gain better access to reliable, high-quality inputs. With improved inputs, farmers are likely to expand vegetable cultivation and increase overall production and shift from low-value crops like bitter mustard to higher-value varieties such as broccoli, cauliflower, lettuce, and bok choy. In addition, they are expected to save on input costs and increase their income through improved yields.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Timor Leste, the Market Development Facility (MDF) provided business development support to women entrepreneurs in coffee farming households. In partnership with Cafe Brisa Serena and Olam, 2,217 women (59% of participants) received training, strengthening women&#x2019;s roles in coffee production and on-farm decision-making. MDF also improved access to coffee processing equipment through partnerships with Karst and Coperativa Comercio Agricultura de Timor, distributing drying racks to 105 women who can now dry their coffee and produce higher quality beans. These women also gained access to drying nets, and pulping machines. MDF also supported the establishment of wet mill and drying facilities through Cafe Organica Atsabe. 16 women accessed these facilities in 2024, enabling them to sell ripe coffee cherries at premium prices and improve their income. I lead a group of 18 farmers, including women, and together we are now using drying racks and pulping machines to produce better [product]. Many of us are selling cherries at higher prices, earning more for our families, and playing a stronger role in farm decisions. This support is giving us the tools and confidence to build a better future in coffee&#x201d; - Anita de Jesus, coffee farmer and extensionist, Aileu Municipality.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">MDF in collaboration with Cottonii Group, trained seaweed farmers in Atauro, Timor-Leste, on efficient farming and drying methods. This led to increased productivity and better-quality seaweed. Seaweed is a climate sustainable crop requiring almost no land or irrigation, cultivation process and reduces greenhouse gases. The program also facilitated market access, enabling farmers to sell additional produce and earn more income. One of the farmers, Otniel Mameia (who has mobility challenges), was able to finance his son&#x2019;s education with the additional income. In 2023, the program benefitted 894 farmers, generating additional income of USD 53,186 (AUD79,779) and USD 99,971 (AUD149,956.50)* export revenue for the country. The intervention also increased the participation of women and persons with disabilities in seaweed farming. *difference in amounts is due to income factoring in operational costs and exports representing sale price.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">MDF supported two local businesses to setup Agri-input shops in remote areas, these shops also share valuable information with men and women farmers, resulting in increases in vegetable farming in those areas. One shop has partnerships with Indonesian input suppliers which has created a competitive market through increased supply and better prices. As a result of readily available agricultural inputs (fertilizers etc), farmers have seen an increase in productivity. This has also led to an increase in the variety of vegetables available and farmers have been able to expand their farms. Farmers no longer need to travel to buy agricultural inputs, and as a result they reported savings of USD55 (AUD 83) per year on travel expense that they now use for other household expenses. MDF partners have generated over USD48,000 (AUD72,000) collectively from input sales.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
